   "id": "exam-explain",
   "label": "Exam-Style Question: Two Kinds of Error",
   "type": "self-marked-response",
   "containerId": "y9l26-exam-explain",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam-Style Question: Two Kinds of Error</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">This question has 2 marks. What do you need to say?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>One point about a syntax error (breaks the rules, will not run) and one about a logic error (runs, wrong result).</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Use the words syntax and logic in your answer.</p><div id=\"y9l26-exam-explain\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "id": "two-kinds",
     "prompt": "Describe the difference between a syntax error and a logic error. [2]",
     "marks": 2,
     "modelAnswer": "A syntax error breaks the rules of the programming language, so the program will not run. A logic error does not stop the program running, but it gives the wrong result."
    }
   ]
